From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 05:53:10 +0200
Subject: MIPS: size ZONE_DMA to the uncached window on Intel MIPS

There is no ISA bus on this SoC and nothing that needs the first 16 MiB
of memory. What it does have is a constraint of exactly the shape
ZONE_DMA exists to express: coherent allocations can only come from the
first 256 MiB of DRAM, because that is all the uncached alias covers.
Point the zone at that instead, and the allocator can place those
allocations by itself rather than every driver finding the bound the
hard way.

MAX_DMA_ADDRESS gets a platform arm, in the shape the file already uses
for SGI IP22/IP28. PAGE_OFFSET is 0x80000000 and PHYS_OFFSET is
0x20000000 here, so PAGE_OFFSET + SZ_256M gives MAX_DMA_PFN 0x30000 and
a ZONE_DMA of physical 0x20000000-0x2FFFFFFF -- the uncached window,
exactly.

The second half is easy to miss. dma-direct decides which zone to ask
from by comparing a device's limit against zone_dma_limit, and that
variable defaults to DMA_BIT_MASK(24) with a comment saying the arch
must override it if its ZONE_DMA is somewhere else. MIPS never has. The
default is accidentally correct on a platform whose MAX_DMA_ADDRESS is
the ISA bound and whose PHYS_OFFSET is zero, and wrong on any other --
so it silently became wrong here the moment the zone moved.

Rather than assign a second constant that can drift from the first,
derive it from MAX_DMA_PFN in the one place that consumes MAX_DMA_PFN.
The two then cannot disagree by construction.

That derivation is generic, so it is worth saying precisely who it
touches. ZONE_DMA is only selected on MIPS by GENERIC_ISA_DMA where ISA
DMA is not marked broken, which is four platforms: MACH_JAZZ, SNI_RM,
MIPS_MALTA and this one. The first three, and Malta without EVA, have
PHYS_OFFSET 0 and the ISA MAX_DMA_ADDRESS, so the derivation yields
DMA_BIT_MASK(24) and nothing changes. Malta under EVA has PAGE_OFFSET 0
and PHYS_OFFSET 0x80000000, where it yields 0x80FFFFFF -- which is where
that config's ZONE_DMA genuinely is, against a default describing an
address range containing no memory at all. IP22/IP28 mark ISA DMA broken
and build with no ZONE_DMA, so the whole block compiles out.

The vendor kernel makes the same move, and makes it family-wide rather
than per board. AVM's 4.9 tree for this silicon enables CONFIG_ZONE_DMA
on every GRX500 member -- it arrives by SOC_GRX500 selecting
GENERIC_ISA_DMA, with CONFIG_ISA deliberately left unset so the ISA
branch of the coherent-allocation path compiles out -- and sizes the
zone to the window through a Kconfig knob, MAX_ZONEDMA_SIZE, whose range
stops at 256 and which the shipping FRITZ!Box 7590 configuration sets to
128. Nothing here needs a knob: one platform, and the whole window is
the right answer for it. Taking all 256 MiB rather than the vendor's
half is a strictly larger reserve, and it splits the zones exactly where
the bound is rather than short of it.

The same sizing then applies to the 256 MiB GRX350 boards, and costs
them nothing: their DRAM ends at the top of the window, so ZONE_DMA is
all of memory and ZONE_NORMAL is simply empty. Worth stating because it
is the one place this could have been a silent change to a board that
was already proven. The vendor ships the identical mechanism on those
boards too -- 128 MiB there, which does split the zones -- so a zone
split on this family is vendor-attested rather than invented, and what
the GRX350 gets here is the degenerate case of it.

This only makes the zone describe the window. It does not yet put
anything in it -- a coherent allocation still lands wherever the buddy
allocator happens to have pages, since no device on this SoC has a mask
narrow enough to be routed to ZONE_DMA by itself.

